WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Calculation of a Six Sigma Performance Baseline

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.600
Welcome to this session on establishing baselines.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.000
What we’re looking at here, is that we have several CTQ’s.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
CTQ 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Now, with respect to each of these CTQ’s, there is a unit of product.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.300
Each unit of product produced, where rows shows units.

7
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.700
So, we have unit 1, unit 2, 3, 4, 5, and so on.

8
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.700
Now, in this instance of unit 1, we can see that it has five CTQ’s.

9
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.100
Thus, every unit produced has the same number of CTQ’s

10
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.066
and the same identical CTQ’s.

11
00:00:52.066 --> 00:00:58.466
Also, there is one opportunity for each type of CTQ.

12
00:00:58.466 --> 00:01:04.899
What we’re seeing tabulated is the outcome of production.

13
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:11.900
In other words, we have five CTQ’s in unit 1, all five passed.

14
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:17.700
If we saw a one, say for example, in cell location C11.

15
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.733
If there was a one, that is to say that CTQ was defective.

16
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:27.233
But since we observe a zero, in this particular cell.

17
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:34.199
Right there, since we observed zero, then we know that it was a pass.

18
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.900
Now, how do we get that?

19
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.733
If we go directly up to the yellow, we see Sigma capability.

20
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:49.033
This is the short-term capability for each of the corresponding CTQ’s.

21
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:55.266
So for CTQ 1, we see that it has a short-term capability of Three Sigma.

22
00:01:55.266 --> 00:02:00.132
That converts to a yield of 93.32%.

23
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:06.266
We define that by the equation normsdist of C6,

24
00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:09.532
which is three Sigma minus 1.5.

25
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:15.399
We subtract the 1.5 to convert the short-term Sigma to a long-term yield,

26
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.366
which is 93.32%.

27
00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:24.499
So, for each of the five CTQ’s, we start with an assumption of three Sigma capability.

28
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:30.166
Where each CTQ has a yield of 93.32%.

29
00:02:30.166 --> 00:02:36.666
If we hit F9, we can initiate the simulation, as many times as we would like.

30
00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:43.332
What we will be doing in the simulation is producing 1000 units of product.

31
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:47.333
Where each unit of product, has the five CTQ’s.

32
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:54.366
And we are looking at the inspection and test outcome for each of those CTQ’s.

33
00:02:54.366 --> 00:02:58.066
If I see a zero in column I,

34
00:02:58.066 --> 00:03:03.766
then I know at least one of the CTQ’s did not conform to standard.

35
00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:07.766
Now, let's look at how we got the simulation.

36
00:03:07.766 --> 00:03:12.666
We have, If a uniform random number between zero and one,

37
00:03:12.666 --> 00:03:18.099
is less than the defect rate, one minus C7,

38
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:24.166
then print a 1, otherwise print zero.

39
00:03:24.166 --> 00:03:30.299
Thus, if the random number is less than one minus the yield,

40
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.866
then I see a defective.

41
00:03:33.866 --> 00:03:38.699
A defective CTQ. In this case, CTQ 1 is defective.

42
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.933
Now, in the result I say, if some of C11 to G11, which is from here to here.

43
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:47.966
C11 to G11.

44
00:03:47.966 --> 00:03:54.132
If that sum is equal to zero, then a 1, that's to say it would be a yield.

45
00:03:54.133 --> 00:04:01.099
So, I want to create a counter for yield conditions, for all 1000.

46
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.366
And you can see if hitting F9,

47
00:04:05.366 --> 00:04:09.366
I can replicate the thousand over and over.

48
00:04:09.366 --> 00:04:15.899
Now, we come up to cell location B3, and we see the output.

49
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.666
Hitting F9, changing it.

50
00:04:18.666 --> 00:04:24.299
In this case, I have 695 ones.

51
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:30.100
In other words, I had 695 cases, units,

52
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.766
in which all CTQ’s passed, out of a thousand.

53
00:04:35.766 --> 00:04:40.532
That would be a throughput yield of 69.5%,

54
00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:45.866
which converts to 92.98% per CTQ.

55
00:04:45.866 --> 00:04:52.832
We find that, by taking the throughput yield, raising it to one over the number of CTQ’s.

56
00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:58.666
Converting the normalized yield to Sigma, using normsinv

57
00:04:58.666 --> 00:05:04.666
of the normalized yield, and then adding back the 1.5 Sigma shift.

58
00:05:04.666 --> 00:05:07.832
We see the overall Sigma,

59
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:14.133
or the kind of like an average Sigma capability per CTQ.

60
00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:18.133
So, this is the aggregate that has been normalized.

61
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:24.166
Running our simulation over and over, we can see that it's Three Sigma.

62
00:05:24.166 --> 00:05:28.932
Which we would expect, because all of the inputs was Three Sigma.

63
00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:34.899
Again, looking at the Sigma capability.

64
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:38.833
Looking at that Sigma capability constantly changing,

65
00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:45.299
2.99, 3.02, 2.94. As we see that Sigma capability change,

66
00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.633
the average will come out, over many, many cycles,

67
00:05:50.633 --> 00:05:54.633
to exactly Three Sigma. Which we would expect.

68
00:05:54.633 --> 00:05:58.966
This is one of the ways that we can perform simulation,

69
00:05:58.966 --> 00:06:05.366
in this case we have simulated the baseline of a particular process.

70
00:06:05.366 --> 00:06:10.366
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
